From 945ccf7b7fcd6364f02f9189b4ca1fe1c992ede5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 18:29:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cgroup: report "frozen 0" in cgroup.events on kernels without
 the v2 freezer

Since v256, systemd reacts to a cgroup.events inotify event by reading the
file and asking for both the "populated" and the "frozen" key
(unit_check_cgroup_events() in src/core/cgroup.c). cg_get_keyed_attribute()
returns ENXIO when any requested key is missing, and the caller then
returns without queueing the cgroup-empty event. The "frozen" key arrived
with the cgroup v2 freezer in 5.2.

On a kernel without the freezer the effect is that systemd never learns
that a cgroup became empty unless it also gets a SIGCHLD for the unit's
main process. Plain services stop normally, but every scope unit (login
sessions, systemd-run --scope) and user@.service, whose cgroups hold no
child of PID 1, hang in "stop" until TimeoutStopSec expires, and a
container shutdown that has a login session open takes the full timeout
and then a SIGKILL.

Print "frozen 0" after "populated" exactly as 5.2 does. Nothing can be
frozen through cgroup2 on this kernel, so the value is always right, and
cgroup.freeze stays absent, so systemd still reports the freezer as
unsupported for "systemctl freeze".

Observed on a Samsung Galaxy A01 (4.9.227) running Fedora 44 (systemd 259)
and Arch (systemd 262) as Droidspaces guests. A 4.14 tree that carries
Google's Android R freezer backport does not need this; one that does not
(Google's own android-4.14-stable included) does.
---
 kernel/cgroup.c | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/kernel/cgroup.c b/kernel/cgroup.c
index 805afbe1b8a..254a70edc90 100644
--- a/kernel/cgroup.c
+++ b/kernel/cgroup.c
@@ -3535,6 +3535,13 @@ static int cgroup_events_show(struct seq_file *seq, void *v)
 {
 	seq_printf(seq, "populated %d\n",
 		   cgroup_is_populated(seq_css(seq)->cgroup));
+	/*
+	 * This kernel has no cgroup v2 freezer, so nothing is ever frozen.
+	 * Say so the way 5.2 does: systemd 256 and newer parse this file for
+	 * both keys and drop the whole notification, including the populated
+	 * change it came for, when either is missing.
+	 */
+	seq_puts(seq, "frozen 0\n");
 	return 0;
 }
 
